{"name":"Cobalt Blue Tarantula","hex":"#02367d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cobalt","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chemical element (symbol Co) with an atomic number of 27: a hard, lustrous, silver-gray metal.","Cobalt blue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cobalt"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"tarantula","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the large, hairy, mostly New World spiders comprising the family Theraphosidae.","A member of certain other groups of spiders, generally characterized by large size, hairiness, or membership of infraorder Mygalomorphae to which Theraphosidae family also belongs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tarantula"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cobalt blue tarantula","blocks":[{"text":"The cobalt blue tarantula or Cyriopagopus lividus is a species of tarantula which is in the family Theraphosidae which is native to Myanmar and over the border into Thailand. It was originally described as Haplopelma lividum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Haplopelma_lividum%2C_Cobalt_blue_tarantula_cropped.jpg/330px-Haplopelma_lividum%2C_Cobalt_blue_tarantula_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobalt_blue_tarantula","wikidata":"Q134978"},{"title":"Cobalt blue","blocks":[{"text":"Cobalt blue is a blue pigment consisting principally of cobalt(II) aluminate, CoAl2O4, made by sintering cobalt(II) oxide with aluminium(III) oxide (alumina) at high temperatures. It is used as a ceramic color, in automotive and industrial coatings, in scientific and medical imaging, and as a fine-art pigment. Its thermostability, chemical inertness, and lightfastness make it suitable for applications requiring a durable blue color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Cobalt_Blue.JPG/330px-Cobalt_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobalt_blue","wikidata":"Q425278"},{"title":"Cobalt","blocks":[{"text":"Cobalt is a chemical element; it has symbol Co and atomic number 27. As with nickel, cobalt is found in the Earth's crust only in a chemically combined form, save for small deposits found in alloys of natural meteoric iron. The free element, produced by reductive smelting, is a hard, lustrous, somewhat brittle, gray metal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Kobalt_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Kobalt_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobalt","wikidata":"Q740"},{"title":"Tarantula","blocks":[{"text":"Tarantulas are a group of large and often hairy spiders of the family Theraphosidae. More than a thousand species have been identified, within almost 200 genera. The term \"tarantula\" is usually used to describe members of the family Theraphosidae, although many other members of the same infraorder (Mygalomorphae) are commonly referred to as \"tarantulas\" or \"false tarantulas\". Some of the more common species have become popular in the exotic pet trade. Many New World species kept as pets have setae known as urticating hairs that can cause irritation to the skin, and in extreme cases, cause damage to the eyes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Brachypelma_vagans_p1.jpg/330px-Brachypelma_vagans_p1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarantula","wikidata":"Q213383"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Haplopelma_lividum_-_captive_breeding.jpg/120px-Haplopelma_lividum_-_captive_breeding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Haplopelma_lividum_-_captive_breeding.jpg/250px-Haplopelma_lividum_-_captive_breeding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Haplopelma_lividum_-_captive_breeding.jpg/120px-Haplopelma_lividum_-_captive_breeding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHaplopelma_lividum_-_captive_breeding.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/2iH_lividum.JPG/120px-2iH_lividum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/2iH_lividum.JPG/250px-2iH_lividum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/2iH_lividum.JPG/120px-2iH_lividum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2iH_lividum.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Blue_and_white_vase_1271_1368_Jingdezhen_unearthed_in_Jiangxi_Province.jpg/120px-Blue_and_white_vase_1271_1368_Jingdezhen_unearthed_in_Jiangxi_Province.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Blue_and_white_vase_1271_1368_Jingdezhen_unearthed_in_Jiangxi_Province.jpg/250px-Blue_and_white_vase_1271_1368_Jingdezhen_unearthed_in_Jiangxi_Pro